    "english": "In Korean traditional cosmology, fire embodies the Wood-Fire-Earth-Metal-Water (오행, Five Elements) system's fire element, representing life force and concentrated yang energy (양기). Fire is associated with the south direction, summer, and intense vitality — a roaring fire dream is read as a surge of this yang energy, an auspicious portent. Korean shamanism (무속신앙) has long used fire as a purifying and protective force: in gut (무당 ritual) ceremonies, fire burns away malevolent spirits and misfortune while inviting good fortune. Folk customs like burning straw bales on the first full moon (달집태우기) and burning rice paddy borders (논두렁 태우기) express this same worldview — fire cleanses the old and makes room for new blessings. The seemingly paradoxical belief that a house burning in a dream is good luck directly stems from this 'fire equals purification equals renewal' equation. Across East Asian cultures, bright fire similarly signals prosperity and honor; Chinese traditional dream interpretation aligns closely with the Korean reading. A uniquely Korean interpretive key, however, is the focus on the dreamer's felt sensation: whether one feels heat and pain in the dream is the primary determinant of whether the fire dream is auspicious or inauspicious."
