    "english": "In Korean traditional culture, the watermelon has long been regarded as a symbol of abundance, fertility, and life force. From the Joseon dynasty onward, watermelon was a prized fruit placed on ancestral rite tables (차례상), and its large, round shape packed with numerous seeds was associated with the flourishing of children and family prosperity. The deep red flesh was interpreted as representing vitality and life energy, while the thick rind symbolized protection of inner richness from the outside world. In the tradition of pregnancy dreams (태몽), watermelon holds a particularly prominent place — dreaming of holding or receiving a large, beautiful watermelon is one of the classic auspicious omens foretelling the birth of a healthy, wealthy child. Because it is a sweet fruit, watermelon is sometimes linked to a daughter, but a large and solid watermelon is also broadly interpreted as portending a remarkable person regardless of gender. Within Korean dream interpretation, watermelon dreams rank alongside pig and rice dreams as some of the most powerfully auspicious food-related omens, consistently linked to wealth luck and good fortune."
