    "english": "In Korean traditional culture, mirrors were far more than practical objects — they carried sacred, shamanic, and political power. Shamans (무당) carried mirrors as one of their three essential ritual tools alongside the sword and bells, using them for divination to locate missing persons and lost objects. Ancient bronze mirrors were placed in royal tombs as symbols of a ruler's authority and magical power. Historical records tell of Goryeo's founder Wang Geon gaining the courage to establish his kingdom from inscriptions on an ancient mirror, while Joseon's founder Yi Seonggye reportedly interpreted a dream of a breaking mirror as an auspicious omen for a new beginning. Folk beliefs held that receiving a mirror in a dream foretold the birth of a son, while reflecting another person in a mirror predicted news arriving from far away. Mirrors also served as tokens of identity and commitment between separated lovers, who would split a mirror and use the reassembled pieces to verify each other's identity upon reunion — underscoring the mirror's deep association with truth, fate, and personal identity in Korean cultural consciousness."
